IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `wra-core`

-- Welfare Reform Act 2012 (United Kingdom), 2012 c. 5, Part 5, ss 98 to 104: claims and awards, payments, appeals,
-- electronic communications. Layer 1 (the law, objectively), part wra4.
-- Most of these sections amend the Social Security Administration Act 1992 (SSAA) and the Social Security Act 1998
-- (SSA1998) to give regulations new powers; those powers are machinery (inert, reason 2). What is encoded is the
-- inserted text that a person meets: what regulations may require of them (SSAA 5(1A)), how joint payment may be
-- directed (5(3B)), the payment-on-account cases (5(1)(r)), the appeal rules (SSA1998 12(2)(b), (3A)-(3C), (7)(b)) and
-- the reading rules of ss 103 and 104. The host Acts are not among the sources: what they say elsewhere is a borrowed fact.
-- Result convention: JUST FALSE / FALSE = the text says no; NOTHING = not covered (see wra4-ss96-97.l4).

§ `s 99 -- information relating to claims and awards`

-- Inserted SSAA 5(1A): regulations may require a person "of a prescribed description" to supply information that is, or
-- could be, relevant to a claim or award, or to potential claims or awards. The description is for regulations.
-- src:1612 | (1A) Regulations may make provision for requiring a person of a prescribed description to supply any information or evidence which is, or could be, relevant to—
-- src:1613 | (a) a claim or award relating to a benefit to which this section applies, or
-- src:1614 | (b) potential claims or awards relating to such a benefit.
@ref s 99(3) SSAA 5(1A) (src:1612-1614)
GIVEN `the person is of a description regulations prescribe` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN  @desc Are you of a description that the regulations prescribe as one who must supply information? Leave out if not known.
      `the information is or could be relevant to a claim or award, or to potential claims or awards` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Is the information one that is, or could be, relevant to a claim or award of the benefit, or to a possible later claim or award?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 99(3) SSAA 5(1A) regulations may require the person to supply the information` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the person is of a description regulations prescribe`
    WHEN JUST TRUE THEN
        IF `the information is or could be relevant to a claim or award, or to potential claims or awards`
        THEN JUST TRUE
        ELSE NOTHING                                      -- NOTHING-kind (limited by own wording): the power reaches information "relevant to" a claim, award, or potential claim or award
    WHEN JUST FALSE THEN NOTHING                          -- NOTHING-kind (limited by own wording): the power is for "a person of a prescribed description"; the text does not say no to anyone else
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING                             -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): "a person of a prescribed description"

§ `s 100 -- payments to joint claimants`

-- Inserted SSAA 5(3B): for a benefit awarded to persons jointly, the Secretary of State may be given power to determine
-- to which of them a payment is made: (a) whichever they nominate, or (b) one of them whatever they nominate.
DECLARE `s 100 SSAA 5(3B) way of directing a joint payment` IS ONE OF
    `s 100 SSAA 5(3B)(a) to whichever of them they nominate`
    `s 100 SSAA 5(3B)(b) to one of them irrespective of any nomination`

-- src:1620 | (3B) The power in subsection (1)(i) above to make provision for the person to whom a benefit is to be paid includes, in the case of a benefit awarded to persons jointly, power to make provision for the Secretary of State to determine to which of them all or any part of a payment should be made, and in particular for the Secretary of State—
-- src:1621 | (a) to determine that payment should be made to whichever of those persons they themselves nominate, or
-- src:1622 | (b) to determine that payment should be made to one of them irrespective of any nomination by them.
@ref s 100 SSAA 5(3B) (src:1620-1622)
GIVEN `the benefit is awarded to persons jointly` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Is the benefit awarded to you and another person jointly?
      `the way of directing the payment` IS A `s 100 SSAA 5(3B) way of directing a joint payment`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 100 SSAA 5(3B) the Secretary of State may be given power to direct the payment in this way` MEANS
    IF `the benefit is awarded to persons jointly`
    THEN (CONSIDER `the way of directing the payment`
          WHEN `s 100 SSAA 5(3B)(a) to whichever of them they nominate` THEN JUST TRUE
          WHEN `s 100 SSAA 5(3B)(b) to one of them irrespective of any nomination` THEN JUST TRUE)
    ELSE NOTHING                                          -- NOTHING-kind (limited by own wording): "in the case of a benefit awarded to persons jointly"

§ `s 101 -- payments on account`

-- Substituted SSAA 5(1)(r): regulations may provide for a payment on account (i) where it is impracticable to determine
-- or pay at once, (ii) in cases of need, or (iii) where the Secretary of State considers, in accordance with prescribed
-- criteria, that the payment can reasonably be expected to be recovered.
-- src:1625 | (r) for the making of a payment on account of such a benefit—
-- src:1626 | (i) in cases where it is impracticable for a claim to be made or determined immediately, or for an award to be determined or paid in full immediately,
-- src:1627 | (ii) in cases of need, or
-- src:1628 | (iii) in cases where the Secretary of State considers in accordance with prescribed criteria that the payment can reasonably be expected to be recovered;
@ref s 101(1) SSAA 5(1)(r) (src:1625-1628)
GIVEN `it is impracticable for a claim to be made or determined, or an award determined or paid in full, immediately` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Is it impracticable for your claim to be made or decided immediately, or for your award to be decided or paid in full immediately?
      `the case is one of need` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Are you in need?
      `the Secretary of State considers, in accordance with prescribed criteria, that the payment can reasonably be expected to be recovered` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Does the Secretary of State consider, by the prescribed criteria, that a payment on account to you can reasonably be expected to be recovered?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 101(1) SSAA 5(1)(r) regulations may provide for a payment on account in this case` MEANS
    IF `it is impracticable for a claim to be made or determined, or an award determined or paid in full, immediately`
       OR `the case is one of need`
       OR `the Secretary of State considers, in accordance with prescribed criteria, that the payment can reasonably be expected to be recovered`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING                                          -- NOTHING-kind (list not closed): the three cases are those regulations may provide for; the text is silent on other cases

§ `s 102 -- consideration of revision before an appeal`

-- Inserted SSA1998 12(2)(b): a right of appeal "where regulations under subsection (3A) so provide".
-- src:1636 | (b) where regulations under subsection (3A) so provide.
@ref s 102(2) SSA1998 12(2)(b) (src:1636-1636)
GIVEN `regulations under subsection (3A) so provide` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN  @desc Do regulations under SSA 1998 s 12(3A) give you a right of appeal in your case? Leave out if not known.
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 102(2) SSA1998 12(2)(b) there is a right of appeal because regulations so provide` MEANS
    CONSIDER `regulations under subsection (3A) so provide`
    WHEN JUST TRUE THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN JUST FALSE THEN NOTHING                          -- NOTHING-kind (limited by own wording): paragraph (b) is one source of a right of appeal; paragraph (a) and the host Act's s 12(2) are not among the sources
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING                             -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): regulations are not among the sources

-- Inserted SSA1998 12(3A) (seam 8, PLAN.md section 8): where regulations so provide there is a right of appeal in relation
-- to a decision "only if" the Secretary of State has considered whether to revise it under section 9.
-- src:1638 | (3A) Regulations may provide that, in such cases or circumstances as may be prescribed, there is a right of appeal under subsection (2) in relation to a decision only if the Secretary of State has considered whether to revise the decision under section 9.
@ref s 102(3) SSA1998 12(3A) (src:1638-1638)
GIVEN `the regulations require consideration of revision before an appeal` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN  @desc Do regulations require the Secretary of State to have considered revising the decision before you can appeal it? Leave out if not known.
      `the Secretary of State has considered whether to revise` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Has the Secretary of State considered whether to revise the decision (SSA 1998 s 9)?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 102(3) SSA1998 12(3A) there is a right of appeal` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the regulations require consideration of revision before an appeal`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING                             -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): regulations are not among the sources
    WHEN JUST FALSE THEN NOTHING                          -- NOTHING-kind (silent): without such regulations the right comes from SSA 1998 s 12(2), which is not among the sources
    WHEN JUST TRUE THEN
        IF `the Secretary of State has considered whether to revise`
        THEN NOTHING                                      -- NOTHING-kind (limited by own wording): "only if" is a necessary condition; the text does not say what else gives the right
        ELSE JUST FALSE                                   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "there is a right of appeal ... only if the Secretary of State has considered whether to revise the decision"

-- Inserted 12(3B): regulations "may in particular provide that that condition is met only where" (a) the consideration was
-- on an application, (b) the Secretary of State considered issues of a specified description, or (c) any other condition.
DECLARE `s 102(3) SSA1998 12(3B) further condition` IS ONE OF
    `s 102(3) SSA1998 12(3B)(a) the consideration was on an application`
    `s 102(3) SSA1998 12(3B)(b) the Secretary of State considered issues of a specified description`
    `s 102(3) SSA1998 12(3B)(c) the consideration satisfied another condition the regulations specify`

-- src:1639 | (3B) The regulations may in particular provide that that condition is met only where—
-- src:1640 | (a) the consideration by the Secretary of State was on an application,
-- src:1641 | (b) the Secretary of State considered issues of a specified description, or
-- src:1642 | (c) the consideration by the Secretary of State satisfied any other condition specified in the regulations.
@ref s 102(3) SSA1998 12(3B) (src:1639-1642)
GIVEN `the further condition regulations set` IS A MAYBE `s 102(3) SSA1998 12(3B) further condition`  @desc Do the regulations say the consideration counts only if it was on an application, only if it dealt with issues of a specified description, or only if it met another condition? Leave out if they say nothing or this is not known.
      `the consideration satisfied that condition` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Did the Secretary of State's consideration of whether to revise meet that condition?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 102(3) SSA1998 12(3B) the consideration meets the condition the regulations set` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the further condition regulations set`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING                             -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): the further conditions are for regulations ("may in particular provide")
    WHEN JUST c THEN
        IF `the consideration satisfied that condition`
        THEN JUST TRUE
        ELSE JUST FALSE                                   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "that condition is met only where" (a), (b) or (c)

-- Inserted 12(3C): the references in (3A) and (3B) to regulations and to the Secretary of State are subject to any
-- enactment that transfers the functions to another person.
-- src:1643 | (3C) The references in subsections (3A) and (3B) to regulations and to the Secretary of State are subject to any enactment under or by virtue of which the functions under this Chapter are transferred to or otherwise made exercisable by a person other than the Secretary of State.
@ref s 102(3) SSA1998 12(3C) (src:1643-1643)
GIVEN `an enactment transfers the functions under this Chapter to another person` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Has an enactment transferred the Secretary of State's functions in this matter to another person?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 102(3) SSA1998 12(3C) the references to the Secretary of State and regulations are read as the other person's` MEANS
    IF `an enactment transfers the functions under this Chapter to another person`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING                                          -- NOTHING-kind (limited by own wording): the references are "subject to any enactment ... transferred"; without one the text says nothing

-- Inserted 12(7)(b): where in accordance with regulations under (3A) there is no right of appeal, any purported appeal
-- "may be treated as an application for revision under section 9". The regulations must provide for it.
-- src:1646 | (b) after that paragraph there is inserted—
-- src:1647 | (b) provide that, where in accordance with regulations under subsection (3A) there is no right of appeal against a decision, any purported appeal may be treated as an application for revision under section 9.
@ref s 102(4) SSA1998 12(7)(b) (src:1646-1647)
GIVEN `there is no right of appeal in accordance with regulations under subsection (3A)` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Do the regulations say you have no right of appeal against this decision?
      `regulations provide for a purported appeal to be treated as an application for revision` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN  @desc Do regulations provide that your purported appeal is treated as an application for revision? Leave out if not known.
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 102(4) SSA1998 12(7)(b) the purported appeal may be treated as an application for revision` MEANS
    IF `there is no right of appeal in accordance with regulations under subsection (3A)`
    THEN (CONSIDER `regulations provide for a purported appeal to be treated as an application for revision`
          WHEN JUST TRUE THEN JUST TRUE
          WHEN JUST FALSE THEN NOTHING                    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by own wording): the paragraph is a power to provide; without such regulations the text is silent
          WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING)                      -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): regulations are not among the sources
    ELSE NOTHING                                          -- NOTHING-kind (limited by own wording): the paragraph applies "where ... there is no right of appeal"

§ `s 103 -- supersession of decisions of former appellate bodies`

-- s 103(2): the Schedule 12 amendments have effect as if in force on 3 November 2008 (and so do regulations made under the
-- powers those amendments confer, if the regulations so provide). Before that day the text says nothing.
-- src:1662 | (2) The following have effect as if they had come into force on 3 November 2008—
-- src:1663 | (a) the amendments made by Schedule 12, and
@ref s 103(2)(a) (src:1662-1663)
GIVEN `the day` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 103(2)(a) the Schedule 12 amendments have effect on the day` MEANS
    IF `the day` AT LEAST (Date 3 11 2008)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING                                          -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the text says what the position is from 3 November 2008, not before it

-- src:1662 | (2) The following have effect as if they had come into force on 3 November 2008—
-- src:1664 | (b) if regulations made in the exercise of the powers conferred by virtue of those amendments so provide, those regulations.
@ref s 103(2)(b) (src:1662-1664)
GIVEN `the regulations so provide` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN  @desc Do the regulations made under the powers of Schedule 12 say that they have effect from 3 November 2008? Leave out if not known.
      `the day` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 103(2)(b) the regulations have effect on the day` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the regulations so provide`
    WHEN JUST TRUE THEN
        IF `the day` AT LEAST (Date 3 11 2008)
        THEN JUST TRUE
        ELSE NOTHING                                      -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the text speaks of effect from 3 November 2008 only
    WHEN JUST FALSE THEN NOTHING                          -- NOTHING-kind (limited by own wording): "if regulations ... so provide"; the text does not say what follows if they do not
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING                             -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): regulations are not among the sources

§ `s 104 -- electronic communications: how references in the Electronic Communications Act 2000 are read`

-- Inserted SSAA 189(5B) and SSA1998 79(6B): in a case where regulations require or authorise electronic communications,
-- references in ss 8(4), 8(5) and 9(5) of the Electronic Communications Act 2000 to an order under section 8 are read as
-- references to regulations under the Act, and references to anything authorised by such an order as references to
-- anything required or authorised by such regulations.
DECLARE `s 104 reference in the Electronic Communications Act 2000` IS ONE OF
    `s 104 a reference to an order under section 8`
    `s 104 a reference to anything authorised by such an order`
    `s 104 any other reference`

DECLARE `s 104 how the reference is read` IS ONE OF
    `s 104 read as a reference to regulations under the Act`
    `s 104 read as a reference to anything required or authorised by such regulations`

-- src:1669 | (5B) For the purposes of subsection (5A), references in section 8(4) and (5) and 9(5) of the Electronic Communications Act 2000 to an order under section 8 of that Act are to be read as references to regulations under this Act; and references to anything authorised by such an order are to be read as references to anything required or authorised by such regulations.
@ref s 104(1) SSAA 189(5B) (src:1669-1669)
GIVEN `the reference` IS A `s 104 reference in the Electronic Communications Act 2000`
GIVETH A MAYBE `s 104 how the reference is read`
`s 104(1) SSAA 189(5B) the reference is read as` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the reference`
    WHEN `s 104 a reference to an order under section 8` THEN JUST `s 104 read as a reference to regulations under the Act`
    WHEN `s 104 a reference to anything authorised by such an order` THEN JUST `s 104 read as a reference to anything required or authorised by such regulations`
    WHEN `s 104 any other reference` THEN NOTHING         -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the subsection reads two kinds of reference and no other

-- src:1672 | (6B) For the purposes of subsection (6A), references in section 8(4) and (5) and 9(5) of the Electronic Communications Act 2000 to an order under section 8 of that Act are to be read as references to regulations under this Act; and references to anything authorised by such an order are to be read as references to anything required or authorised by such regulations.
@ref s 104(2) SSA1998 79(6B) (src:1672-1672)
GIVEN `the reference` IS A `s 104 reference in the Electronic Communications Act 2000`
GIVETH A MAYBE `s 104 how the reference is read`
`s 104(2) SSA1998 79(6B) the reference is read as` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the reference`
    WHEN `s 104 a reference to an order under section 8` THEN JUST `s 104 read as a reference to regulations under the Act`
    WHEN `s 104 a reference to anything authorised by such an order` THEN JUST `s 104 read as a reference to anything required or authorised by such regulations`
    WHEN `s 104 any other reference` THEN NOTHING         -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the subsection reads two kinds of reference and no other
